────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
10. COMMON OPERATIONS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Run the award check manually (outside of scheduled time):
  cd core && php artisan award:check-qualifications

View scheduled jobs:
  cd core && php artisan schedule:list

Inspect queue jobs (if using database queue):
  SELECT * FROM jobs ORDER BY created_at DESC;
  SELECT * FROM failed_jobs ORDER BY failed_at DESC;

Retry failed award jobs:
  php artisan queue:retry all

View the cron log:
  tail -f core/storage/logs/award-check.log

Seed a test award (example):
  INSERT INTO awards
    (name, required_total_pv, required_left_pv, required_right_pv,
     payment_amount, sort_order, status, created_at, updated_at)
  VALUES
    ('Bronze', 3000, 1500, 1500, 50000, 1, 1, NOW(), NOW());


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
11. IMPORTANT BEHAVIOURS & EDGE CASES
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  a. Awards are evaluated in sort_order / required_total_pv order.
     If a user qualifies for multiple awards in one job run, all of
     them are created in sequence. Higher awards can therefore be
     earned in the same nightly run as lower awards, provided the
     prerequisites are already met or were created earlier in that
     same run (in-memory chaining via $earnedAwardIds[]).

  b. An inactive award (status=0) is never evaluated. Deactivating
     an award that some users have already earned does NOT revoke
     those UserAward records.

  c. Deleting a prerequisite award sets prerequisite_award_id = NULL
     on any award that referenced it (ON DELETE SET NULL), effectively
     removing the prerequisite requirement for those awards going
     forward.

  d. The BFS in getLegUserIds is capped at 1,000 users per leg.
     For trees larger than 1,000 deep/wide, the prerequisite check
     may not scan the entire leg. Increase the cap if the network
     grows significantly.

  e. The job has $tries = 3 and $timeout = 120 seconds. A job
     processing a user in a very large tree may time out. If this
     occurs repeatedly, consider increasing $timeout or breaking
     the BFS into a separate pre-computation step.

  f. withoutOverlapping() on the schedule means: if the 02:00 run
     is still dispatching jobs at 02:00 the next day, the second
     invocation is silently skipped. The dispatching itself is fast
     (just INSERT into jobs table) so this is unlikely in practice,
     but the jobs themselves run asynchronously and may still be
     processing when the next schedule fires.
